  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 126 PM EDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis:\n\nStrong troughing with its axis currently through Ontario and the \nUpper Midwest will punch overhead this evening and tonight, quickly \nvacating to the east by Thursday morning. Ample forcing aloft \ngenerated by this wave will continue to support the associated \nsurface cyclone as it pivots across Hudson Bay and into northern \nQuebec. Subsidence on the backside of this system will build surface \nhigh pressure into the region through the second half of this week \nand into this weekend. Troughing looks to swing back overhead by the \nsecond half of the weekend with a cold front potentially working \nacross the area in the late Saturday/early Sunday timeframe. While \nstill several days away, this appears to be the start of a more \nprogressive pattern next week with high pressure in place for a \nshort period of time Monday before another wave progresses over the \nGreat Lakes by midweek.\n\nForecast Details:\n\nWith rain showers moving off to our southeast this afternoon, dry \nweather will set in across the Northwoods this evening through at \nleast until the end of the week -- and perhaps a several day stretch \ninto next week for some areas. The cold front lagging this morning's \nactivity will eventually swing south of the area later today with \nnorthwest winds advecting a cooler, drier airmass into northern \nMichigan on Thursday. Afternoon temperatures in the mid 60s to low \n70s are in store with mostly sunny skies. With little sensible \nweather over the next few days, perhaps the main focus through the \nremainder of the work week will be the chilly temperatures that are \nanticipated Thursday night into Friday morning. High pressure \noverhead will set up ideal radiational cooling with clear skies and \ncalm winds, potentially allowing localized low-lying areas within \nthe higher terrain to dip into the upper 30s and low 40s with \nwidespread 40s and low 50s elsewhere.\n\nAs discussed by the overnight forecaster, the aforementioned wave \nexpected to punch across us this weekend appears it will be moisture \nstarved and may struggle to generate widespread rain/thunder chances \nacross the area. Isolated to scattered activity is most likely later \nSaturday into early Sunday, but there's potential most areas remain \nrain-free this weekend into early next week. Highs in the 70s and \neven low 80s will be several degrees above average as we head into \nthe middle of September.",
